From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Give explorer's taskbar a Windows 10 appearance, in place

The shell strategy (ADR 0007) keeps Wine's explorer as the shell process so
applications keep working: Shell_TrayWnd owns the tray protocol, the taskbar
buttons, the taskbar position and ITaskbarList. The Windows 10 look is added
by upgrading that bar in place rather than drawing a second one over it or
hiding it -- every part of the shell protocol is untouched, only how the bar
looks and what it shows.

The changes, all in programs/explorer/systray.c:

  - height raised to the Windows 10 40px (tray_height);
  - a flat dark bar (WM_ERASEBKGND fills it; the class background brush is
    dropped);
  - flat task buttons instead of the 3D DrawFrameControl frame -- the app icon
    and title, with a purple accent underline on the active window;
  - the Start button drawn as the project's own stained-glass mark, four
    diamond tiles in purple, magenta, amber and turquoise, replacing the
    trophy-and-"Start" caption;
  - a clock (time over date) at the right, with the button layout reserving
    its width.

No protocol path changes, so applications see the same shell. Verified: the
session gate passes with the taskbar now 1280x40 and both 32- and 64-bit
apps launching into it.

Forwarded: not-needed
--- a/programs/explorer/systray.c
+++ b/programs/explorer/systray.c
